description: Always deliver action plans as plain-text, copy-paste-ready output
alwaysApply: true
---

# Action plan output format

Whenever a response includes an action plan, execution plan, roadmap, or
step-by-step rollout (e.g. a client's N-day SEO plan, a launch checklist, a
task list meant to be handed off or pasted elsewhere), always present it as
plain text ready to copy-paste — not as rendered markdown with links,
checkboxes styled as UI widgets, or wiki-style `[[...]]`/`[text](path)` links.

Formatting rules for this plain-text output:
- Use a wrapped plain-text/code block (triple backticks, no language tag) so
  it renders as literal copy-pasteable text in chat.
- Use simple ASCII structure: dashed section rules, numbered tasks, plain
  parenthetical owner/effort tags — no markdown headers (`#`), bold (`**`),
  or links inside the block.
- Strip internal wiki cross-references (file paths, `[[page]]` links) from
  this output — those belong in the underlying wiki page, not the
  copy-paste version.
- This applies regardless of whether the plan was just discussed in-chat or
  pulled from an existing wiki page (e.g. `wiki/clients/<slug>/*.md`) — the
  underlying wiki file keeps full markdown/links as normal; only the
  chat-facing plan output follows this plain-text rule.
- This is a standing preference for this project: apply it by default to
  every future action-plan output without being re-asked.

## Client/prospect-facing text (proposals, cold emails, Upwork bids) — stricter rules

Anything meant to be sent externally (an Upwork proposal, a cold email, a
message to a prospect or client) must read like a human typed it directly
into the send box, ready to select-all, copy, and paste with zero edits.
This is stricter than the general action-plan format above:

- No `[ ]` or `[x]` checkbox brackets. These read as internal task-tracker
  syntax, not something a person writes in a proposal. Use plain numbers
  ("1.", "2.") only.
- No hard line breaks in the middle of a sentence or paragraph. Write each
  numbered item's body as one continuous line (let the chat UI/recipient's
  textbox soft-wrap it naturally) rather than manually wrapping at ~70
  characters. Manually-wrapped text looks fine in a code block but pastes
  into Upwork/Gmail/etc. with awkward mid-sentence breaks that scream
  "pasted from an AI tool."
- No other bracketed meta-notation (`[Owner]`, `[TBD]`, placeholders in
  brackets, etc.) — write it as real words or omit it.
- Keep estimates and asides as plain parenthetical or trailing sentences,
  not a separate bracketed tag.
- Before finalizing, mentally re-read it as "would this look identical to
  something typed by hand in a text box" — if any artifact (brackets, odd
  breaks, markdown leftovers) would survive a copy-paste, remove it.

### Length limit (Upwork cover letters) — hard rule

- Upwork cover letters are capped at 5,000 characters including spaces. Never
  deliver a draft over that. Aim for 4,500-4,800 so there is a buffer, and
  never leave less than ~100 characters of margin.
- Measure, don't guess: before presenting a cover letter, write it to a temp
  file outside the repo and count characters with a shell command
  (`(Get-Content -Raw file).TrimEnd().Length` in PowerShell). State the
  character count right after the letter, e.g. "4,900 / 5,000".
- If a draft is over the limit, cut in this order and never touch the first
  two: (1) never cut price, timeline, or the direct answers to the client's
  numbered questions; (2) never cut the client's-need opening or the video
  link. Then trim: credibility commentary and self-description first, then
  the 6-month/3-month results narrative, then per-item deliverable detail
  (collapse into one line per month or phase), then extra offers.
- Use short forms where it saves room and still reads naturally ("GBP" after
  the first mention, "Fre and Eng" is acceptable in a plan list, "Q and A").
- Other Upwork-style text (bids, short messages) follows the same
  "measure, then state the count" habit when the platform has a limit.

### Tone: less categorical, less padded (learned from the human's edit of the Groupe Nova letter, 2026-10-19)

The human cut an over-length, over-confident AI draft down to a final 4,900
character letter and softened it. Apply these by default to every future
proposal:

- Do not assert what you do not know. When the scope depends on something the
  client has not said (how many ads, how many landing pages, which pages are
  needed), state the assumption in plain words instead of presenting a fixed
  plan as fact: "I do not know how many ads you will run - for my plan I
  assumed one ad per region in both French and English."
- Be honest about gaps in one short line, then move on. Say "I do not have a
  Quebec renovation site I can point you to" once; do not add defensive
  explanations, reassurances, or "what transfers" paragraphs beyond one
  sentence.
- Drop self-promotional filler: no "I am based in X so I work in this market,"
  no "which is the kind of durable work I aim for," no "I am responsible for
  everything that goes live," no "I have already built this kind of listing
  set..." re-statements, no comparisons to other freelancers ("anyone who
  promises you #1 is selling...").
- Keep personal details minimal. Mention "a native Quebec French speaker"
  reviews the French; do not describe the household or relationship.
- Do not enumerate every place the client named (all six regions, every town)
  when "several local regions" or a short group says the same thing. Name
  specifics only where they change the work.
- Results sections stay short: "No ranking guarantees," then 2-3 plain
  sentences per time horizon. No promises about compounding, competing, or
  dominating, and no "should be able to see" claims beyond what the plan
  actually delivers.
- Credibility proof is a list of links plus one sentence. Do not narrate
  stats or long-term ranking history unless the client asks for it.
- State the currency once, in caps, right before the first price ("Please
  note that ALL PRICES ARE IN USD").
- Open with the client's need (not a greeting or thanks), then the video
  link, then answer the client's numbered questions in the order they were
  asked, using their own numbering and short labels.
- Offers that add length (extra sample videos, extra calls) are optional:
  keep one at most, and only if there is room under the limit.
